/*
 * HamGeek / RIGUKE XC7Z100 + ADRV9009 ("sdr-pro-alpha") default environment.
 *
 * Board-bootloader policy §2 "Boot menu (interruptible)": the default boot must
 * be interruptible into an interactive bootmenu by pressing ESC
 * (CONFIG_AUTOBOOT_MENUKEY=27) during a 1 s autoboot delay, and the menu must
 * offer the recovery actions so a wedged unit needs no external tools.
 *
 * Modelled on board/libre-computer/common/boot.env. Divergences, all forced by
 * this board's hardware rather than chosen:
 *
 *  - No Shutdown entry: Zynq-7000 has no PMIC poweroff path reachable from
 *    U-Boot (CONFIG_CMD_POWEROFF has no provider here), so a "Shutdown" item
 *    would be a dead menu row.
 *  - No splash entries: the board has no display output at all (see the §2 row
 *    in design/rules/bootloader/board-compliance.md in the claude repo).
 *  - "USB SD Mode" rather than "USB eMMC Mode": there is no eMMC connector on
 *    this carrier; the SD on mmc 0 is the only MMC device.
 *  - EtherealOS / Firmware Update rows are compiled in only when their
 *    machinery exists (CONFIG_WGET / EFI capsule), so the menu never advertises
 *    an action that cannot run. Both are still open findings for this board.
 *
 * menucmd is what common/autoboot.c runs when the menu key is seen
 * (autoboot.c:506), and the while-loop is what makes the menu LOOP until an
 * action, per policy §2. "Console" clears bootmenu_run, which drops to the
 * U-Boot prompt unauthenticated -- deliberate, per policy §2/§6.
 */
menucmd=env set bootmenu_run true; while $bootmenu_run; do bootmenu -1; done
/*
 * THE NUMBERING MUST BE CONTIGUOUS. cmd/bootmenu.c builds the menu with
 *
 *     while ((option = bootmenu_getoption(i)))
 *
 * (prepare_bootmenu_entry(), cmd/bootmenu.c:203), i.e. it stops at the FIRST
 * absent bootmenu_<n> and silently discards every higher-numbered entry. So an
 * #ifdef'd-out row in the MIDDLE of the range truncates the menu there.
 *
 * That is not hypothetical: with CONFIG_WGET unset, the EtherealOS row at
 * bootmenu_3 was absent, and the menu this board actually rendered was
 *
 *     1. Boot
 *     2. Select Boot
 *     3. UEFI Config
 *
 * and nothing else -- USB SD Mode, USB DFU Mode, Firmware Update, Console and
 * Reboot all existed as environment variables and NONE of them was reachable.
 * The recovery UI policy §2 asks for was, in practice, three rows. Confirming
 * the variables individually with printenv could never have caught it; only the
 * rendered menu shows it.
 *
 * Therefore: every UNCONDITIONAL row comes first, in one unbroken run, and the
 * OPTIONAL rows are the tail, spelled out per combination so the numbering is
 * contiguous in all four. If an optional row is compiled out, the only thing
 * lost is that row -- never a recovery action.
 */
bootmenu_0=Boot=run bootcmd
bootmenu_1=Select Boot=bootflow scan -mtb; pause "Boot Selection Failed. Press any key for menu."
bootmenu_2=UEFI Config=eficonfig
bootmenu_3=USB SD Mode=if mmc dev 0; then echo "\n  *** USB SD Mode ***\n"; ums 0 mmc 0; else pause "\n  *** SD Not Found ***\n"; fi
bootmenu_4=USB DFU Mode=echo "\n  *** USB DFU Mode ***\n"; dfu 0 sf 0; pause
bootmenu_5=Console=env set bootmenu_run false
bootmenu_6=Reboot=reset
/*
 * The Firmware Update row must LOAD a capsule before applying one. The Libre
 * Computer boards fetch it (dhcp; wget http://boot.libre.computer/ci/$board.cap)
 * -- this board has no CONFIG_WGET and no slug in that namespace, so the source
 * is the ESP instead. Applying $loadaddr directly, as this row did when it was
 * first written, applies whatever happens to be in DRAM at loadaddr, which on
 * this board is 0x0 (CONFIG_SYS_LOAD_ADDR=0): the row could only ever have
 * printed "Firmware Update Failed". It was never harmful -- an unsigned or
 * unparseable blob is rejected before any flash write -- but it was never
 * useful either.
 *
 * mmc 0:1 is the ESP, the same partition uboot.env lives on (U-Boot names it
 * explicitly when the file is missing: 'Unable to read "uboot.env" from
 * mmc0:1'). capsuleaddr is 0x10000000, clear of the SPL BSS/stack and of the
 * relocated U-Boot; loadaddr itself is deliberately not used.
 */
capsuleaddr=0x10000000
capsulefile=xc7z100-adrv9009.cap
#if defined(CONFIG_EFI_HAVE_CAPSULE_SUPPORT) && defined(CONFIG_WGET)
bootmenu_7=Firmware Update=if load mmc 0:1 $capsuleaddr $capsulefile; then if efidebug capsule update -v $capsuleaddr; then pause "Firmware Updated Successfully"; else pause "Firmware Update Failed"; fi; else pause "Capsule not found: $capsulefile on the ESP"; fi
bootmenu_8=EtherealOS=pause "EtherealOS is not configured for this board."
#elif defined(CONFIG_EFI_HAVE_CAPSULE_SUPPORT)
bootmenu_7=Firmware Update=if load mmc 0:1 $capsuleaddr $capsulefile; then if efidebug capsule update -v $capsuleaddr; then pause "Firmware Updated Successfully"; else pause "Firmware Update Failed"; fi; else pause "Capsule not found: $capsulefile on the ESP"; fi
#elif defined(CONFIG_WGET)
bootmenu_7=EtherealOS=pause "EtherealOS is not configured for this board."
#endif
/*
 * NOTE: script_offset_f is deliberately NOT defined here, and there is no
 * working place to define it either -- this board has no NOR boot script.
 *
 * Board-bootloader policy §4 gives SPI NOR three regions (bootloader blob,
 * config sector at 0x1F0000, DT overlay FIT at 0x200000) and no boot-script
 * region; per-unit configuration is the config sector's key=value overrides.
 * So CONFIG_BOARD_CONFIG drops the qspi boot target that would consume
 * the variable (include/configs/zynq-common.h) and suppresses the
 * env_set_hex("script_offset_f", CONFIG_BOOT_SCRIPT_OFFSET) fallback in
 * board/xilinx/common/board.c. Nothing sets it and nothing reads it.
 *
 * History, because two earlier fixes were aimed at the wrong layer: the value
 * was first put here, which is INERT (board.c overwrites the default env on
 * every boot -- measured 2026-07-31, a board with the value compiled into
 * env.in and no saved uboot.env still reported upstream's ARCH_ZYNQ default
 * fc0000). It was then moved to CONFIG_BOOT_SCRIPT_OFFSET=0x200000 in the
 * defconfig, which worked but aimed the reader at the overlay-FIT region.
 * The right answer was that the consumer does not belong on this board.
 */
